Stencicrity for Debian
======================

What is in the package
----------------------

Stencicrity is a Rust program. The .deb carries one binary,
/usr/bin/stencicrity, with every crate it uses linked in statically. At run
time it needs nothing but the C runtime, so dh_shlibdeps resolves the whole
dependency list to libc6 and libgcc-s1 and the package installs on any
release with a new enough glibc, on any architecture Rust targets.

xdg-utils is a Recommends, not a Depends: it is only used to open the preview
PNG in the desktop image viewer, and the program prints the path and carries
on when xdg-open is missing.

Building
--------

The source is native (debian/source/format is "3.0 (native)"): the Debian
packaging lives in the upstream git tree and there is no separate upstream
tarball. dpkg-buildpackage only looks at ./debian, while the packaging is
kept in packaging/debian next to the Arch PKGBUILD, so stage a copy first:

  cp -a packaging/debian debian
  dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b -d

Building needs cargo and rustc 1.85 or newer on the PATH. They are not listed
in Build-Depends, because the toolchain is taken from rustup rather than from
the archive - trixie's rustc is exactly 1.85 and a build-dependency on it
would pin the package to one release's compiler. That is what the -d is for:
it tells dpkg-buildpackage to skip the build-dependency check, which would
otherwise fail over a cargo that is on the PATH but not dpkg-installed. On a
machine where cargo does come from the archive, drop the -d.

cargo is pointed at debian/cargo for its registry cache (CARGO_HOME in
debian/rules), so a build writes nothing outside the source tree. The build
downloads the crates named in Cargo.lock, so it needs network access; the
versions are pinned with --locked, so nothing is resolved differently from
what upstream tested. "debian/rules clean" removes debian/cargo again.

The release workflow in .github/workflows/release.yml does exactly the above
inside a debian:trixie container: it installs rustup non-interactively, sets
the changelog version from the git tag with dch, and builds with -d.

